BASIC HTML REVIEW
=================

1. HTML BASICS
==============

HTML
----

HTML = HyperText Markup Language

HTML provides the CONTENT and STRUCTURE of a webpage.

HTML is made up of elements such as:

- headings
- paragraphs
- links
- images
- lists
- sections
- buttons
- audio
- video


HTML ELEMENTS
-------------

Most HTML elements have:

1. Opening tag
2. Content
3. Closing tag

Syntax:

<element>Content</element>

Example:

<p>This is a paragraph.</p>

Opening tag:

<p>

Closing tag:

</p>


VOID ELEMENTS
-------------

Void elements do not contain content and do not have closing tags.

Examples:

<img>
<meta>
<link>
<input>

You may sometimes see a slash at the end:

<img />

Both styles are generally accepted:

<img>

<img />


ATTRIBUTES
----------

Attributes provide additional information or configuration for an element.

Attributes are placed inside the opening tag.

Syntax:

<element attribute="value">Content</element>

Example:

<a href="https://example.com">Visit Example</a>

Here:

a       = element
href    = attribute
URL     = attribute value


BOOLEAN ATTRIBUTES
------------------

Boolean attributes are either present or absent.

Present = true
Absent  = false

Examples:

disabled
readonly
required
controls
muted
loop
autoplay
allowfullscreen

Example:

<input required>

The presence of "required" enables the behavior.


HTML COMMENTS
-------------

Comments are notes inside your HTML source code.

They are not displayed on the webpage.

Syntax:

<!-- This is a comment -->


2. COMMON HTML ELEMENTS
=======================

HEADINGS
--------

HTML provides six heading levels:

<h1>
<h2>
<h3>
<h4>
<h5>
<h6>

h1 = highest importance
h6 = lowest importance

Example:

<h1>Main Page Title</h1>
<h2>Major Section</h2>
<h3>Subsection</h3>


PARAGRAPH
---------

Use <p> for paragraphs.

Example:

<p>This is a paragraph.</p>


IMAGE
-----

Use <img> to display an image.

Important attributes:

src = location of the image
alt = alternative description of the image

Example:

<img src="dog.jpg" alt="A brown dog playing outside">

The alt attribute is important for accessibility and should
describe meaningful images.


BODY
----

The <body> contains the visible content of the HTML document.

Example:

<body>
    <h1>My Website</h1>
    <p>Welcome to my website.</p>
</body>


SECTION
-------

<section> divides related content into logical sections.

Example:

<section>
    <h2>About Me</h2>
    <p>I am learning HTML.</p>
</section>


DIV
---

<div> is a generic container.

It does not provide specific semantic meaning.

Example:

<div>
    <h2>News</h2>
    <p>Today's news...</p>
</div>

Divs are commonly used for:

- grouping elements
- CSS layouts
- JavaScript behavior


ANCHOR / LINK
-------------

Use <a> to create hyperlinks.

The href attribute specifies the destination.

Example:

<a href="https://www.freecodecamp.org">
    Visit freeCodeCamp
</a>


UNORDERED LIST
--------------

<ul> creates a bulleted list.

Each item uses <li>.

Example:

<ul>
    <li>HTML</li>
    <li>CSS</li>
    <li>JavaScript</li>
</ul>


ORDERED LIST
------------

<ol> creates a numbered list.

Example:

<ol>
    <li>Open Sublime Text</li>
    <li>Create index.html</li>
    <li>Write HTML</li>
</ol>


EMPHASIS
--------

<em> adds semantic emphasis to text.

Example:

<p>I <em>really</em> like HTML.</p>


STRONG IMPORTANCE
-----------------

<strong> indicates strong importance, seriousness, or urgency.

Example:

<p>
    <strong>Warning:</strong> Save your work first.
</p>


FIGURE AND FIGCAPTION
---------------------

<figure> groups self-contained media/content.

<figcaption> provides a caption for it.

Example:

<figure>
    <img src="mountain.jpg" alt="Mountain at sunrise">
    <figcaption>Sunrise over the mountains.</figcaption>
</figure>


MAIN
----

<main> represents the primary content of the page.

Example:

<main>
    <h1>HTML Tutorial</h1>
    <p>Learn HTML fundamentals.</p>
</main>


FOOTER
------

<footer> usually appears near the bottom of a page or section.

It commonly contains:

- copyright information
- contact information
- navigation links
- legal links

Example:

<footer>
    <p>Copyright 2026 Example Company</p>
</footer>


BUTTON
------

<button> creates a clickable button.

Example:

<button>Click Me</button>


3. IDENTIFIERS AND GROUPING
===========================

ID
--

The id attribute uniquely identifies an element.

Example:

<h1 id="page-title">My Website</h1>

An ID should normally appear only ONCE in an HTML document.

IDs cannot contain spaces.

Multiple words can be separated with:

- hyphens
- underscores

Examples:

id="page-title"

id="page_title"


CLASS
-----

Classes group elements together.

Example:

<div class="card"></div>

Unlike IDs, the same class can be used multiple times.

Example:

<div class="card"></div>
<div class="card"></div>
<div class="card"></div>


MULTIPLE CLASSES
----------------

An element can have multiple classes separated by spaces.

Example:

<div class="card featured"></div>

This element has two classes:

card
featured


ID VS CLASS
-----------

ID:

- identifies one unique element
- normally used once per page

Class:

- groups elements
- can be reused
- an element can have multiple classes


4. SPECIAL CHARACTERS AND EXTERNAL RESOURCES
============================================

HTML ENTITIES
-------------

Some characters have special meaning in HTML.

HTML entities allow these characters to be displayed as text.

Examples:

&lt;      displays <
&gt;      displays >
&amp;     displays &
&quot;    displays "
&apos;    displays '

Example:

<p>The &lt;img&gt; element displays an image.</p>

Browser displays:

The <img> element displays an image.


LINK ELEMENT
------------

The <link> element connects an HTML document to an external resource.

A common use is loading CSS.

Example:

<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">

Important attributes:

rel  = relationship between the resources
href = location of the resource


SCRIPT ELEMENT
--------------

<script> adds or loads JavaScript.

JavaScript can be written directly inside:

<script>
    alert("Hello!");
</script>

However, JavaScript is commonly stored in an external file.

Example:

<script src="script.js"></script>

src = location of the JavaScript file


5. HTML BOILERPLATE AND ENCODING
================================

A basic HTML document:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>My Website</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>

<body>

    <h1>My Website</h1>

</body>

</html>


DOCTYPE
-------

<!DOCTYPE html>

Tells the browser that the document uses modern HTML.


HTML ELEMENT
------------

<html> is the root or top-level element.

Example:

<html lang="en">

lang="en" specifies that the document language is English.


HEAD
----

<head> contains information ABOUT the page.

Most content inside <head> is not displayed as normal page content.

Common elements inside <head>:

<title>
<meta>
<link>


BODY
----

<body> contains the content displayed on the webpage.


META
----

<meta> provides metadata about the webpage.

Examples include:

- character encoding
- SEO descriptions
- social-media information


TITLE
-----

<title> determines the text shown in the browser tab.

Example:

<title>Devin's HTML Notes</title>


CHARACTER ENCODING
------------------

UTF-8 is the standard character encoding commonly used on the web.

Use:

<meta charset="utf-8">

Character encoding tells the computer how characters should be
represented and interpreted.


6. SEO AND SOCIAL SHARING
=========================

SEO
---

SEO = Search Engine Optimization

SEO is the practice of making webpages easier for search engines
to understand and discover.


META DESCRIPTION
----------------

A meta description provides a short description of the webpage.

Example:

<meta
    name="description"
    content="Learn the fundamentals of HTML and web development."
>

The description can help search engines understand the page.


OPEN GRAPH
----------

Open Graph metadata controls how a webpage may appear when shared
on social-media platforms.

Open Graph tags are placed inside <head>.


OG:TITLE
--------

Sets the title used when sharing the page.

Example:

<meta property="og:title" content="HTML Fundamentals">


OG:TYPE
-------

Describes the type of content.

Example:

<meta property="og:type" content="website">

Possible content types include things such as:

- website
- article
- video
- music


OG:IMAGE
--------

Specifies the image used when the page is shared.

Example:

<meta
    property="og:image"
    content="https://example.com/preview.jpg"
>


OG:URL
------

Specifies the canonical URL associated with the shared content.

Example:

<meta
    property="og:url"
    content="https://example.com"
>


7. MEDIA ELEMENTS AND OPTIMIZATION
=================================

REPLACED ELEMENTS
-----------------

A replaced element displays content that comes from an external
resource.

Examples include:

<img>
<iframe>
<video>
<embed>

The browser replaces the HTML element with the external content.


IFRAME
------

iframe = inline frame

An iframe embeds another HTML resource within the current page.

Example:

<iframe
    src="https://example.com"
    title="Example website">
</iframe>


IFRAME ATTRIBUTES
-----------------

Common iframe attributes include:

src
title
width
height
allow
referrerpolicy
allowfullscreen

Example:

<iframe
    src="video-url"
    title="Video player"
    width="560"
    height="315"
    allowfullscreen>
</iframe>


ALLOWFULLSCREEN
---------------

allowfullscreen is a boolean attribute.

It allows iframe content such as a video to enter fullscreen mode.


EMBEDDING YOUTUBE VIDEO
-----------------------

YouTube videos normally use an EMBED URL rather than the standard
watch URL.

Example structure:

<iframe
    width="560"
    height="315"
    src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID"
    title="YouTube video player"
    allowfullscreen>
</iframe>


INPUT TYPE IMAGE
----------------

An <input> can behave as a replaced element when its type is image.

Example:

<input
    type="image"
    src="button.png"
    alt="Submit"
>


MEDIA OPTIMIZATION
------------------

Three important considerations when optimizing media:

1. File size
2. File format
3. Compression

Smaller optimized media files can improve page-loading performance.


IMAGE FORMATS
-------------

Common traditional formats:

PNG
JPG / JPEG

Modern optimized formats include:

WEBP
AVIF

Modern formats can often provide smaller files while maintaining
good image quality.


IMAGE LICENSES
--------------

Make sure you have permission to use an image.

Examples of licenses or categories include:

Public Domain

    Generally not restricted by copyright.

Creative Commons Zero / CC0

    Intended to place works in the public domain.

Creative Commons

    May allow use under specific conditions.

Other permissive licenses

    Usage depends on the terms of the license.


SVG
---

SVG = Scalable Vector Graphics

SVG graphics are based on:

- paths
- coordinates
- mathematical shapes

Because SVG is vector-based, it can be enlarged or reduced without
the normal quality loss associated with raster images.


8. AUDIO AND VIDEO
==================

AUDIO
-----

Use <audio> to add audio.

Example:

<audio src="song.mp3"></audio>

Common audio formats include:

MP3
WAV
OGG


CONTROLS
--------

The controls boolean attribute displays browser playback controls.

Example:

<audio src="song.mp3" controls></audio>

Controls can allow users to:

- play
- pause
- resume
- change volume
- seek through media


AUTOPLAY
--------

autoplay tells the browser to begin playing media automatically
when possible.

Example:

<audio src="song.mp3" autoplay></audio>

Important:

Modern browsers frequently restrict autoplay, especially when
audio is not muted.


LOOP
----

loop causes media to repeat.

Example:

<audio src="song.mp3" controls loop></audio>


MUTED
-----

muted causes media to begin muted.

Example:

<audio src="song.mp3" controls muted></audio>


SOURCE ELEMENT
--------------

Different browsers may support different media formats.

Multiple <source> elements can be placed inside <audio> or <video>.

Example:

<audio controls>
    <source src="song.ogg" type="audio/ogg">
    <source src="song.wav" type="audio/wav">
    <source src="song.mp3" type="audio/mpeg">
</audio>

The browser selects a source format it supports.


VIDEO
-----

Use <video> to display video.

Common formats include:

MP4
OGG
WEBM

Example:

<video src="movie.mp4" controls></video>


VIDEO ATTRIBUTES
----------------

Many attributes used with audio also work with video:

controls
autoplay
loop
muted


Example:

<video
    src="movie.mp4"
    controls
    loop
    muted>
</video>


POSTER
------

poster specifies an image displayed while the video is loading
or before playback starts.

Example:

<video
    src="movie.mp4"
    controls
    poster="preview.jpg">
</video>

poster is specific to video and is not an audio attribute.


9. LINK TARGET ATTRIBUTE
========================

The target attribute determines WHERE a link opens.

Example:

<a href="https://example.com" target="_blank">
    Example
</a>


_SELF
-----

target="_self"

Default behavior.

Opens the destination in the current browsing context.

Usually:

current tab/window


_BLANK
------

target="_blank"

Opens the destination in a new browsing context.

Usually:

new browser tab


_PARENT
-------

target="_parent"

Opens the destination in the parent browsing context.

This is particularly relevant when working inside iframes.


_TOP
----

target="_top"

Opens the destination in the highest-level browsing context.

Useful when content is nested inside multiple frames.


EXPERIMENTAL VALUE
------------------

_unfencedTop

This is associated with the experimental FencedFrame API.

It is not something you will normally need in basic HTML.


10. FILE PATHS AND URLs
=======================

PATH
----

A path specifies the location of a file or directory.

Paths are used to locate:

- webpages
- images
- CSS files
- JavaScript files
- fonts
- media


PATH SEPARATOR
--------------

A slash separates folders and files in a path.

Example:

images/photos/cat.jpg


CURRENT DIRECTORY
-----------------

.

A single dot refers to the current directory.

Example:

./styles.css

Meaning:

styles.css is located relative to the current directory.


PARENT DIRECTORY
----------------

..

Two dots refer to the parent directory.

Example:

../styles.css

Meaning:

move up one directory, then find styles.css.


EXAMPLES
--------

public/index.html

./favicon.ico

../css/styles.css


ABSOLUTE PATH
-------------

An absolute path describes the complete route to a resource in a
file system.

It begins from the root of the file system or hierarchy.


ABSOLUTE URL
------------

An absolute URL provides the complete web address.

It generally includes:

protocol
domain
path

Example:

https://example.com/images/logo.svg

Parts:

https://
    protocol

example.com
    domain

/images/logo.svg
    path


RELATIVE PATH
-------------

A relative path specifies a location relative to the current file.

Example:

about.html

If index.html and about.html are in the same folder:

<a href="about.html">About</a>


ABSOLUTE URL VS RELATIVE URL
----------------------------

Absolute:

<a href="https://example.com/about.html">About</a>

Relative:

<a href="about.html">About</a>

Absolute URLs include the complete location.

Relative URLs depend on the location of the current document.


11. CSS LINK STATES
===================

Links can exist in several states.

These states can be selected using CSS pseudo-classes.


:link
-----

A link that has not yet been visited.

Example:

a:link {
    /* styles */
}


:visited
--------

A link the user has previously visited.

Example:

a:visited {
    /* styles */
}

Browsers commonly display visited links differently by default.


:hover
------

Applies while the mouse pointer is over a link.

Example:

a:hover {
    /* styles */
}


:focus
------

Applies when the link receives keyboard or programmatic focus.

Example:

a:focus {
    /* styles */
}

Focus is particularly important for keyboard accessibility.


:active
-------

Applies while the user is actively activating the link.

Usually this occurs while the mouse button is being pressed.

Example:

a:active {
    /* styles */
}


