KEY IDEAS TO REMEMBER
=====================

1. HTML defines content and structure.

2. Most elements have an opening tag and closing tag.

3. Void elements such as <img> and <meta> do not contain content.

4. Attributes provide additional information about elements.

5. <h1> through <h6> represent heading hierarchy.

6. <a> creates links and href specifies their destination.

7. <img> uses src for the image and alt for alternative text.

8. IDs should be unique.

9. Classes can be reused.

10. <head> contains metadata.

11. <body> contains visible page content.

12. UTF-8 is normally declared using:

    <meta charset="utf-8">

13. Meta descriptions can help search engines understand a page.

14. Open Graph metadata affects how links appear when shared.

15. iframe embeds external HTML/media content.

16. audio and video support controls, autoplay, loop, and muted.

17. video additionally supports poster images.

18. Multiple <source> elements can provide alternative media formats.

19. target="_blank" normally opens a link in a new tab.

20. ./ refers to the current directory.

21. ../ refers to the parent directory.

22. Absolute URLs contain the complete web address.

23. Relative paths depend on the current file's location.

24. CSS link states include:
    :link
    :visited
    :hover
    :focus
    :active

25. Modern image formats such as WEBP and AVIF can reduce image
    file size.

26. SVG graphics scale without losing quality.


SEMANTIC HTML REVIEW
====================

SEMANTIC HTML
-------------

Semantic HTML uses elements based on the MEANING and PURPOSE of the content.

Semantic HTML helps:
- organize a page logically
- improve accessibility
- help screen readers understand page structure
- make code easier for developers to understand

Presentational HTML focuses only on appearance.

Old/deprecated presentational elements include:
- <center>
- <big>
- <font>


HEADING STRUCTURE
-----------------

HTML headings range from:

<h1> through <h6>

<h1> = highest-level heading
<h6> = lowest-level heading

Heading levels should follow a logical hierarchy.

Avoid skipping heading levels.

Example:

<h1>Web Development</h1>
    <h2>HTML</h2>
        <h3>Semantic HTML</h3>

Skipping heading levels can make page structure harder for screen readers
and negatively affect accessibility.


PAGE STRUCTURE ELEMENTS
-----------------------

<header>

Represents introductory content for a page or section.

Example:

<header>
    <h1>Developer Blog</h1>
    <p>Learning web development one project at a time.</p>
</header>


<main>

Contains the primary content of the page.

Example:

<main>
    <h1>My Blog</h1>
    <p>Welcome to my website.</p>
</main>


<section>

Groups related content into a thematic section.

Example:

<section>
    <h2>About Me</h2>
    <p>I am learning web development.</p>
</section>


<nav>

Contains navigation links.

Example:

<nav>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="#about">About</a></li>
        <li><a href="#contact">Contact</a></li>
    </ul>
</nav>


<article>

Represents independent, self-contained content.

Good uses:
- blog posts
- news stories
- event entries
- forum posts

Example:

<article>
    <h2>My First Blog Post</h2>
    <p>This is my first article.</p>
</article>


FIGURES
-------

<figure>

Groups an image, illustration, diagram, or similar content with related
information.

<figcaption>

Provides a visible caption for the figure.

Example:

<figure>
    <img src="cat.jpg" alt="Two tabby kittens sleeping together.">
    <figcaption>Two sleeping kittens</figcaption>
</figure>

The <figcaption> belongs INSIDE the <figure>.


TEXT EMPHASIS
-------------

<em>

Represents stress emphasis.

Example:

<p>I <em>really</em> want to learn HTML.</p>


<i>

Represents text that is different from the surrounding prose, such as:
- foreign words
- technical terms
- thoughts
- alternative voice or mood

Example:

<p>The French phrase <i lang="fr">je ne sais quoi</i> is often used in English.</p>

The lang attribute identifies the language.

IMPORTANT:

Do not use <i> or <em> merely because you want italic-looking text.

If the goal is only visual styling, use CSS.


<strong>

Represents strong importance, seriousness, or urgency.

Example:

<p><strong>Warning:</strong> Do not delete this file.</p>


<b>

Draws attention to text without saying that the text is especially important.

Possible uses:
- keywords
- product names
- important terms in a summary

Example:

<p>The <b>SuperSound 3000</b> received the highest rating.</p>


DESCRIPTION LISTS
-----------------

<dl>

Creates a description list.

<dt>

Represents a term.

<dd>

Provides the description or definition.

Example:

<dl>
    <dt>HTML</dt>
    <dd>HyperText Markup Language</dd>

    <dt>CSS</dt>
    <dd>Cascading Style Sheets</dd>
</dl>


QUOTATIONS
----------

<blockquote>

Used for a longer quotation that stands on its own.

The cite attribute can contain the URL of the source.

Example:

<blockquote cite="https://example.com/article">
    This is a longer quotation from another source.
</blockquote>


<q>

Used for short quotations that appear inside normal text.

Example:

<p>Jordan said, <q>Practice every day.</q></p>


<cite>

Represents the title of a referenced creative work.

Examples:
- book title
- article title
- movie title

Do NOT use <cite> simply for a person's job title.


ABBREVIATIONS
-------------

<abbr>

Represents an abbreviation.

Use title to provide the full meaning.

Example:

<p>
    <abbr title="HyperText Markup Language">HTML</abbr>
    is the foundation of a web page.
</p>


CONTACT INFORMATION
-------------------

<address>

Represents contact information for a person or organization.

Example:

<address>
    123 Main Street<br>
    Atlanta, GA 30303
</address>


<br>

Creates a line break.

It can be useful where line breaks are meaningful, such as a mailing address.

Do not use many <br> elements just to create visual spacing.


PHONE LINKS
-----------

Use tel: to create a clickable phone number.

Example:

<a href="tel:+14045550198">(404) 555-0198</a>


EMAIL LINKS
-----------

Use mailto: to create a clickable email address.

Example:

<a href="mailto:hello@example.com">hello@example.com</a>


DATES AND TIMES
---------------

<time>

Represents a date and/or time.

The datetime attribute provides a machine-readable value.

Example:

<time datetime="2026-09-18">September 18, 2026</time>

Date and time together:

<time datetime="2026-09-18T14:00">
    September 18, 2026 — 2:00 PM
</time>


ISO 8601 DATE/TIME FORMAT
-------------------------

A common machine-readable format is:

YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss

Example:

2026-09-18T14:30:00

T separates the date from the time.


SUPERSCRIPT
-----------

<sup>

Displays semantic superscript text.

Common uses:
- exponents
- ordinal notation
- superior lettering

Example:

<p>x<sup>2</sup></p>

Displays:

x²


SUBSCRIPT
---------

<sub>

Displays semantic subscript text.

Common uses:
- chemical formulas
- variable subscripts
- footnote references

Example:

<p>CO<sub>2</sub></p>

Displays:

CO₂


COMPUTER CODE
-------------

<code>

Represents computer code.

Use it for short inline code references.

Example:

<p>The <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> element creates a paragraph.</p>


<pre>

Represents preformatted text.

Whitespace and line breaks inside <pre> are preserved.

For multi-line code examples, combine <pre> and <code>.

Example:

<pre><code>&lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My first page.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>

Remember:

<code> = identifies computer code
<pre>  = preserves formatting


UNARTICULATED ANNOTATION
------------------------

<u>

Represents text with a non-textual annotation.

One example is marking a spelling error.

Example:

<p>The word <u>accomodate</u> is misspelled.</p>

Do NOT use <u> merely because you want text to look underlined.

Use CSS when the goal is purely visual.


RUBY ANNOTATIONS
----------------

<ruby>

Groups text with a pronunciation or explanatory annotation.

Often used with East Asian languages.


<rt>

Contains the pronunciation or annotation.


<rp>

Provides fallback parentheses for browsers that do not properly display
ruby annotations.

Example:

<ruby>
    明日
    <rp>(</rp>
    <rt>Ashita</rt>
    <rp>)</rp>
</ruby>


OUTDATED INFORMATION
--------------------

<s>

Represents information that is no longer accurate or relevant.

Example:

<p>
    <s>The meeting starts at noon.</s>
</p>

<p>
    The meeting has been canceled.
</p>

Do not use <s> merely because you want a strikethrough appearance.


INTERNAL PAGE LINKS
-------------------

Links can jump to another location on the SAME page.

Create the link:

<a href="#about">About</a>

Create the destination:

<section id="about">
    <h2>About</h2>
</section>

The href value:

#about

must match:

id="about"


QUICK SEMANTIC HTML CHEAT SHEET
-------------------------------

<header>
Introductory content

<nav>
Navigation links

<main>
Primary page content

<section>
Related thematic content

<article>
Independent/self-contained content

<figure>
Image, illustration, diagram, etc.

<figcaption>
Caption belonging to a figure

<em>
Stress emphasis

<strong>
Strong importance

<i>
Alternative voice, technical term, foreign phrase, etc.

<b>
Draw attention without adding importance

<blockquote>
Long quotation

<q>
Short inline quotation

<cite>
Title of a referenced work

<abbr>
Abbreviation

<address>
Contact information

<br>
Line break

<time>
Date/time

<sup>
Superscript

<sub>
Subscript

<code>
Computer code

<pre>
Preformatted text

<u>
Non-textual annotation

<ruby>
Ruby annotation container

<rt>
Ruby pronunciation/annotation

<rp>
Ruby fallback parentheses

<s>
No-longer-accurate information


MAIN SEMANTIC HTML RULE
-----------------------

Do not choose an HTML element because of how the browser makes it LOOK.

Choose the element because of what the content MEANS.

Ask yourself:

"What does this content represent?"

Then choose the HTML element whose semantic meaning best matches that content.

HTML TABLES AND FORMS REVIEW
============================


HTML FORMS
==========

FORM ELEMENT
------------

<form>

Used to create an HTML form for collecting user input.

Example:

<form action="/submit" method="post">

    <!-- form controls go here -->

</form>


FORM ATTRIBUTES
---------------

action

Specifies the URL where the form data should be sent.

Example:

<form action="https://example.com/register">


method

Specifies the HTTP method used when sending the form data.

Common values:

get
post

Example:

<form action="https://example.com/register" method="post">


GET VS POST
-----------

GET

Form data is generally sent as part of the URL.

Often used when retrieving/searching for information.


POST

Form data is sent in the HTTP request body.

Often used when submitting information that changes or creates data.


INPUT ELEMENT
=============

<input>

Creates a form control.

The behavior of an input depends largely on its type attribute.

Example:

<input type="text">


COMMON INPUT TYPES
------------------

type="text"

Creates a normal single-line text field.

Example:

<input type="text">


type="email"

Creates an input intended for email addresses.

Browsers can perform basic email-format validation.

Example:

<input type="email">


type="number"

Creates an input for numerical values.

Example:

<input type="number">


type="radio"

Creates a radio button.

Radio buttons are normally used when the user should choose ONE option
from a group.

Example:

<input type="radio">


type="checkbox"

Creates a checkbox.

Checkboxes are normally used when the user may choose ZERO, ONE,
or MULTIPLE options.

Example:

<input type="checkbox">


type="submit"

Creates a control that submits a form.

Example:

<input type="submit" value="Submit">


type="reset"

Resets form controls to their initial values.

Example:

<input type="reset" value="Clear Form">


type="button"

Creates a button with no built-in form behavior.

It is commonly used when custom behavior will later be provided,
usually with JavaScript.

Example:

<input type="button" value="Show Information">


COMMON INPUT ATTRIBUTES
=======================

id

Uniquely identifies an element in the document.

Example:

id="email"


name

Identifies the form field when form data is submitted.

Example:

name="email"


IMPORTANT:

id and name have DIFFERENT purposes.

id
→ identifies the element in the document

name
→ identifies the submitted form field


Example:

<input
    type="email"
    id="email"
    name="email"
>


placeholder

Displays a short hint or example inside an input.

Example:

<input
    type="text"
    placeholder="John Doe"
>


A placeholder should not replace a proper label.


value

Provides a value for a form control.

Example:

<input
    type="text"
    value="Jordan Parker"
>


For buttons, value can determine the visible button text.

Example:

<input
    type="submit"
    value="Register"
>


size

Controls approximately how many characters are visible in certain
text inputs.

Example:

<input
    type="text"
    size="30"
>


FORM LABELS
===========

<label>

Provides descriptive text for a form control.


EXPLICIT LABEL ASSOCIATION
--------------------------

The label's for attribute should match the input's id.

Example:

<label for="email">Email Address:</label>

<input
    type="email"
    id="email"
    name="email"
>


Relationship:

for="email"
        ↓
id="email"


This allows the browser and assistive technologies to determine
which label belongs to which control.


IMPORTANT:

Each id should be UNIQUE within the page.


FORM VALIDATION
===============

HTML provides built-in client-side form validation.


required
--------

Specifies that a field must contain a value before the form can
normally be submitted.

Example:

<input
    type="text"
    required
>


minlength
---------

Specifies the minimum number of characters allowed.

Example:

<input
    type="text"
    minlength="3"
>


maxlength
---------

Specifies the maximum number of characters allowed.

Example:

<input
    type="text"
    maxlength="20"
>


Both can be combined:

<input
    type="text"
    minlength="3"
    maxlength="20"
>


min
---

Specifies the minimum numerical value allowed.

Example:

<input
    type="number"
    min="0"
>


max
---

Specifies the maximum numerical value allowed.

Example:

<input
    type="number"
    max="50"
>


Both can be combined:

<input
    type="number"
    min="0"
    max="50"
>


MULTIPLE VALIDATION RULES
-------------------------

Validation attributes can be combined.

Example:

<input
    required
    type="text"
    id="username"
    name="username"
    minlength="3"
    maxlength="20"
>


This means:

- the field is required
- minimum length is 3 characters
- maximum length is 20 characters


CLIENT-SIDE VALIDATION WARNING
------------------------------

HTML validation helps users catch errors before submission.

It should NOT be treated as a security feature.

Client-side validation can be bypassed.

Applications still need proper server-side validation.


FORM STATES
===========

DEFAULT / EDITABLE
------------------

A normal input can be focused and edited.

Example:

<input
    type="text"
    value="Jordan Parker"
>


READONLY
--------

readonly

Prevents the user from changing the value.

A readonly input can still normally:

- receive focus
- be selected
- have its value submitted with the form

Example:

<input
    readonly
    type="email"
    value="jordan@example.com"
>


DISABLED
--------

disabled

Makes the control unavailable for interaction.

A disabled input normally:

- cannot be edited
- cannot receive focus
- is not submitted with the form

Example:

<input
    disabled
    type="text"
    value="AWS-2026-1048"
>


READONLY VS DISABLED
--------------------

readonly:

visible
focusable
not editable
normally submitted


disabled:

visible
not focusable
not editable
not submitted


FOCUS
-----

A control is focused when it is currently active for user input.

Users can focus controls by:

- clicking them
- tapping them
- navigating with the keyboard, such as Tab

Focus is an important part of keyboard accessibility.


BUTTON ELEMENT
==============

<button>

Creates a button.

Common button types:

submit
reset
button


SUBMIT BUTTON
-------------

<button type="submit">
    Register
</button>

Submits the form.


RESET BUTTON
------------

<button type="reset">
    Clear Form
</button>

Resets the form controls.


GENERIC BUTTON
--------------

<button type="button">
    Show Workshop Info
</button>

Has no built-in form submission behavior.

Often used with JavaScript later.


IMPORTANT BUTTON RULE
---------------------

Specify the button type explicitly.

Example:

<button type="button">

This avoids accidentally submitting a form when a button is meant
for some other behavior.


FIELDSET AND LEGEND
===================

<fieldset>

Groups related form controls together.


<legend>

Provides a caption or description for the fieldset.


Example:

<fieldset>

    <legend>Contact Information</legend>

    <label for="name">Name:</label>
    <input
        type="text"
        id="name"
        name="name"
    >

    <label for="email">Email:</label>
    <input
        type="email"
        id="email"
        name="email"
    >

</fieldset>


Good uses for fieldset:

- radio-button groups
- checkbox groups
- contact-information groups
- logically related form questions


LEGEND VS LABEL
---------------

legend

Describes a GROUP of related controls.


label

Describes a SPECIFIC form control.


Example:

<fieldset>

    <legend>Additional Feedback</legend>

    <label for="comments">
        Additional comments or suggestions:
    </label>

    <textarea
        id="comments"
        name="comments"
    ></textarea>

</fieldset>


RADIO BUTTONS
=============

Radio buttons are used when the user should select ONE option
from a group.

Example:

<fieldset>

    <legend>Was this your first event?</legend>

    <input
        type="radio"
        id="yes"
        name="first-event"
        value="yes"
    >
    <label for="yes">Yes</label>

    <input
        type="radio"
        id="no"
        name="first-event"
        value="no"
    >
    <label for="no">No</label>

</fieldset>


RADIO GROUP RULE
----------------

Radio buttons in the same group share the SAME name.

Example:

name="first-event"


But each radio button should have:

- a unique id
- its own value


Example:

YES

id="yes"
name="first-event"
value="yes"


NO

id="no"
name="first-event"
value="no"


The shared name causes the browser to treat the controls as
one mutually exclusive group.


CHECKBOXES
==========

Checkboxes allow users to select ZERO, ONE, or MULTIPLE options.

Example:

<fieldset>

    <legend>Which sessions did you attend?</legend>

    <input
        type="checkbox"
        id="html"
        name="sessions"
        value="HTML"
    >
    <label for="html">HTML</label>

    <input
        type="checkbox"
        id="forms"
        name="sessions"
        value="HTML Forms"
    >
    <label for="forms">HTML Forms</label>

</fieldset>


CHECKBOX RULES
--------------

Related checkboxes may share the same name.

Example:

name="sessions"


Each checkbox should still have:

- a unique id
- a meaningful value


Example:

id="html"
value="HTML"


id="forms"
value="HTML Forms"


IMPORTANT DISTINCTION
---------------------

Radio buttons:

Choose ONE from a group.


Checkboxes:

Choose ZERO, ONE, or MANY.


SELECT AND OPTION
=================

<select>

Creates a selection list/dropdown.


<option>

Represents one available choice.


Example:

<label for="rating">Workshop Rating:</label>

<select
    id="rating"
    name="rating"
>

    <option value="poor">
        Poor
    </option>

    <option value="good">
        Good
    </option>

    <option value="excellent">
        Excellent
    </option>

</select>


OPTION VALUE
------------

The value attribute represents the value submitted with the form.

Example:

<option value="excellent">
    Excellent
</option>


SELECTED
--------

The selected attribute makes an option initially selected.

Example:

<option
    value="good"
    selected
>
    Good
</option>


TEXTAREA
========

<textarea>

Creates a multi-line text input.

Use textarea when the visitor needs room to enter:

- sentences
- comments
- feedback
- descriptions
- longer messages


Example:

<label for="comments">
    Additional comments:
</label>

<textarea
    id="comments"
    name="comments"
    rows="10"
    cols="50"
></textarea>


Unlike <input>, textarea has both an opening and closing tag.


TABLES
======

HTML tables are used to represent TABULAR DATA.

Tabular data is information that naturally makes sense in
rows and columns.

Examples:

- attendance reports
- exam scores
- schedules
- financial information
- product comparisons
- book catalogs


DO NOT USE TABLES FOR PAGE LAYOUT
---------------------------------

Tables should represent DATA.

Do not use tables simply to position:

- headers
- navigation
- images
- page sections
- footers

Page layout should be handled with appropriate HTML structure and CSS.


BASIC TABLE STRUCTURE
=====================

<table>

Contains the entire table.


Typical structure:

table
├── caption
├── thead
├── tbody
└── tfoot


Example:

<table>

    <caption>Workshop Attendance</caption>

    <thead>
        ...
    </thead>

    <tbody>
        ...
    </tbody>

    <tfoot>
        ...
    </tfoot>

</table>


TABLE CAPTION
=============

<caption>

Provides a title or description for the table.

It belongs directly inside the table.

Example:

<table>

    <caption>
        Workshop Attendance Report
    </caption>

    ...

</table>


TABLE SECTIONS
==============

<thead>

Groups the table's header rows.


<tbody>

Groups the table's primary data rows.


<tfoot>

Groups footer or summary information.

Typical uses:

- totals
- averages
- summary rows


Example:

<table>

    <thead>
        ...
    </thead>

    <tbody>
        ...
    </tbody>

    <tfoot>
        ...
    </tfoot>

</table>


TABLE ROWS
==========

<tr>

Creates a table row.

Example:

<tr>
    ...
</tr>


TABLE HEADER CELLS
==================

<th>

Creates a table header cell.

A header cell identifies or describes other cells.

Example:

<tr>
    <th>Workshop</th>
    <th>Registered</th>
    <th>Attended</th>
</tr>


A <th> is not simply:

"the bold cell"

or:

"the cell at the top"

It has SEMANTIC meaning.

It identifies related table data.


ROW HEADERS
-----------

A <th> can also identify a row.

Example:

<tr>
    <th>HTML Foundations</th>
    <td>40</td>
    <td>36</td>
</tr>


Here:

HTML Foundations

identifies what the numerical data in that row represents.


TABLE DATA CELLS
================

<td>

Creates an ordinary table data cell.

Example:

<tr>
    <th>HTML Foundations</th>
    <td>40</td>
    <td>36</td>
</tr>


In this example:

HTML Foundations
→ header identifying the row

40
→ ordinary data

36
→ ordinary data


TABLE HIERARCHY
===============

A useful mental model:

table

    caption

    thead
        tr
            th
            th
            th

    tbody
        tr
            th
            td
            td

        tr
            th
            td
            td

    tfoot
        tr
            th
            td


COLSPAN
=======

colspan

Specifies how many columns a table cell should occupy.

Example:

<tr>
    <th colspan="2">
        Average Score
    </th>

    <td>89</td>
</tr>


Here:

Average Score

occupies the space of TWO columns.


IMPORTANT:

Do not use colspan simply to make something look wider.

Use it when one piece of data logically represents multiple columns.


TABLE EXAMPLE
=============

<table>

    <caption>
        HTML Workshop Scores
    </caption>

    <thead>

        <tr>
            <th>Participant</th>
            <th>Assessment</th>
            <th>Score</th>
        </tr>

    </thead>

    <tbody>

        <tr>
            <th>Jordan Parker</th>
            <td>Semantic HTML</td>
            <td>92</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <th>Morgan Lee</th>
            <td>Semantic HTML</td>
            <td>88</td>
        </tr>

    </tbody>

    <tfoot>

        <tr>
            <th colspan="2">
                Average Score
            </th>

            <td>90</td>
        </tr>

    </tfoot>

</table>


HTML VALIDATORS
===============

An HTML validator checks markup against HTML standards.

Validators can help detect problems such as:

- invalid nesting
- duplicate IDs
- malformed elements
- incorrect element placement
- missing required attributes
- structural errors


IMPORTANT:

A page may LOOK correct in a browser while still containing
invalid HTML.

Browsers try to recover from malformed markup.


DEVELOPMENT WORKFLOW
--------------------

Write HTML

    ↓

Run validator

    ↓

Read errors and warnings

    ↓

Identify the root cause

    ↓

Correct source HTML

    ↓

Validate again


VALIDATOR ERRORS
----------------

One incorrect piece of HTML can sometimes cause several validator
messages.

Do not assume every message represents a completely separate problem.

Look for the ROOT CAUSE.


DEVTOOLS AND DOM INSPECTOR
==========================

Browser DevTools allow developers to inspect the DOM created by
the browser.

In Chrome:

Right-click an element

→ Inspect


The Elements panel allows you to:

- inspect HTML elements
- examine nesting
- inspect attributes
- temporarily edit elements
- test possible fixes
- debug links and IDs


SOURCE HTML VS DOM
==================

SOURCE HTML

The HTML saved in your file.


DOM

The browser's parsed representation of the document.


These are related, but they are not exactly the same thing.


DEVTOOLS EDITS ARE TEMPORARY
----------------------------

If you edit HTML in DevTools:

the rendered page changes immediately

BUT

your actual index.html file does NOT change.


Example workflow:

1. Inspect an element.

2. Change it temporarily in DevTools.

3. Confirm the change fixes the problem.

4. Update the real index.html file.

5. Save the file.

6. Reload the page.

7. Validate again.


RELOADING THE PAGE
------------------

Changes made only in DevTools normally disappear after a page reload.

Why?

Because the browser rebuilds the DOM from the original source file.


DEBUGGING INTERNAL LINKS
========================

DevTools can help inspect links and their destination IDs.

Example:

<a href="#contact">
    Contact
</a>


Destination:

<section id="contact">
    ...
</section>


These values must correspond:

href="#contact"

id="contact"


