HTML ACCESSIBILITY REVIEW
=========================


INTRODUCTION TO ACCESSIBILITY
=============================

ACCESSIBILITY
-------------

Web accessibility means designing and developing websites so people
with disabilities can perceive, understand, navigate, and interact
with web content.

Accessibility should be considered while building a page rather than
being added only after development is complete.


WCAG
----

WCAG stands for:

Web Content Accessibility Guidelines

WCAG provides guidelines for making web content more accessible.

The four major WCAG principles are remembered with the acronym:

POUR


P = Perceivable
----------------

Users must be able to perceive the information being presented.

Examples:

- text alternatives for images
- captions for video
- transcripts for audio
- content that can be enlarged


O = Operable
------------

Users must be able to operate the interface.

Examples:

- keyboard navigation
- accessible buttons
- logical focus order
- avoiding interactions that require only a mouse


U = Understandable
------------------

Content and interfaces should be understandable.

Examples:

- clear instructions
- understandable labels
- predictable navigation
- descriptive error messages


R = Robust
----------

Content should work with different browsers, devices, and assistive
technologies.

Using valid semantic HTML helps make content more robust.


ASSISTIVE TECHNOLOGY
====================

Assistive technology helps people interact with computers and web
content in different ways.


SCREEN READERS
--------------

Screen readers convert webpage content into speech or other output.

They are commonly used by people who are blind or have significant
visual impairments.

Screen readers can use HTML semantics to identify:

- headings
- links
- buttons
- forms
- tables
- landmarks
- images
- lists


Good semantic HTML provides information that screen readers can use.


LARGE TEXT AND BRAILLE KEYBOARDS
--------------------------------

Specialized keyboards may provide:

- larger keys
- larger printed characters
- braille markings
- simplified layouts

They can help users with visual or motor impairments.


SCREEN MAGNIFIERS
-----------------

Screen magnification software enlarges portions of the screen.

It is commonly used by people with low vision.

Webpages should remain usable when content is enlarged.


ALTERNATIVE POINTING DEVICES
----------------------------

Some users cannot comfortably use a traditional mouse.

Alternative pointing devices can include:

- trackballs
- joysticks
- touchpads
- specialized switches
- other adaptive devices


VOICE RECOGNITION SOFTWARE
--------------------------

Voice recognition software allows users to control a computer using
spoken commands.

It can be especially useful for people with mobility impairments.

Clear link text, labels, and button names can help users activate
controls by voice.


ACCESSIBILITY AUDITING TOOLS
============================

Automated accessibility tools can identify many common accessibility
problems.

Examples include:

Google Lighthouse
WAVE
IBM Equal Access Accessibility Checker
axe DevTools


IMPORTANT:

Automated tools cannot detect every accessibility problem.

Accessibility testing should combine:

automated testing
+
manual inspection
+
keyboard testing
+
assistive technology testing when possible


HEADING STRUCTURE
=================

HTML headings are:

<h1>
<h2>
<h3>
<h4>
<h5>
<h6>


Headings communicate the organization of a document.

They should be chosen based on CONTENT STRUCTURE, not because of how
large or bold they appear.


EXAMPLE
-------

<h1>Web Accessibility Workshop</h1>

<h2>Workshop Topics</h2>

<h3>Semantic HTML</h3>

<h3>ARIA</h3>

<h2>Registration</h2>


This represents a logical hierarchy.


BAD HEADING STRUCTURE
---------------------

Avoid skipping levels simply for visual appearance.

Example:

<h1>Web Accessibility Workshop</h1>

<h3>Registration</h3>


If Registration is a major section under the page heading, an <h2>
would normally be more appropriate.


WHY HEADINGS MATTER
-------------------

Screen reader users may navigate directly between headings.

A logical heading structure allows them to understand and scan the
page more efficiently.


ACCESSIBLE TABLES
=================

Tables should represent tabular information.

Use semantic table elements so assistive technologies can understand
relationships between headers and data.


IMPORTANT TABLE ELEMENTS
------------------------

<table>
Contains the entire table.

<caption>
Provides a title or description for the table.

<thead>
Contains table header rows.

<tbody>
Contains the main table data.

<tfoot>
Contains summary information.

<tr>
Represents a table row.

<th>
Represents a header cell.

<td>
Represents an ordinary data cell.


TABLE CAPTION
-------------

A caption helps users understand the purpose of the table.

Example:

<table>

    <caption>
        Workshop Attendance Results
    </caption>

    ...

</table>


The <caption> should be placed directly inside the <table>, normally
before the table's row groups.


COLUMN HEADERS
--------------

Use:

scope="col"

when a <th> describes the cells beneath it.

Example:

<th scope="col">Employee</th>


ROW HEADERS
-----------

Use:

scope="row"

when a <th> describes the cells beside it.

Example:

<th scope="row">Jordan Parker</th>


MENTAL MODEL
------------

scope="col"

        ↓
        ↓

Data below the header


scope="row"

        → → →

Data beside the header


ACCESSIBLE FORMS
================

Every form control should have an accessible name.

For normal form fields, the preferred solution is usually a native
<label> element.


LABEL AND INPUT RELATIONSHIP
----------------------------

Example:

<label for="email">
    Email Address
</label>

<input
    type="email"
    id="email"
    name="email"
>


Relationship:

label for="email"

        ↓

input id="email"


The values must match.


WHY LABELS MATTER
-----------------

Labels help users understand what information belongs in a form field.

They also improve interaction because clicking the label can focus or
activate the associated form control.


PLACEHOLDERS ARE NOT LABELS
---------------------------

Do not rely on placeholder text as the only description of an input.

Bad:

<input
    type="email"
    placeholder="Email Address"
>


Better:

<label for="email">
    Email Address
</label>

<input
    type="email"
    id="email"
    name="email"
    placeholder="name@example.com"
>


The label identifies the field.

The placeholder may provide an example or hint.


WAI-ARIA
========

WAI-ARIA stands for:

Web Accessibility Initiative -
Accessible Rich Internet Applications


ARIA provides attributes that can communicate additional accessibility
information to assistive technologies.


IMPORTANT ARIA RULE
-------------------

Use native semantic HTML whenever possible.

Prefer:

<button>
    Register
</button>

over attempting to recreate a button using:

<div role="button">
    Register
</div>


Native HTML already provides:

- semantics
- keyboard behavior
- browser behavior
- accessibility information


ARIA should generally supplement HTML rather than replace good HTML.


ARIA ROLES
==========

The role attribute can describe the purpose of an element.

Example:

role="button"


Some categories of ARIA roles include:

- document structure roles
- widget roles
- landmark roles
- live region roles
- window roles
- abstract roles


DOCUMENT STRUCTURE ROLES
------------------------

Describe structural parts of content.


WIDGET ROLES
------------

Describe interactive user interface components.


LANDMARK ROLES
--------------

Identify major areas of a webpage.

Examples of native HTML elements that already create useful landmarks:

<header>
<nav>
<main>
<footer>


When native elements already provide the correct semantics, additional
ARIA roles are usually unnecessary.


LIVE REGION ROLES
-----------------

Used for areas whose content can change dynamically and may need to be
announced to assistive technology.


WINDOW ROLES
------------

Describe window-like interface elements such as dialogs.


ABSTRACT ROLES
--------------

Used internally as part of ARIA's role model.

Developers should not assign abstract roles directly to elements.


ARIA-LABEL
==========

aria-label provides an accessible name directly.

Example:

<button
    type="button"
    aria-label="Search"
>
    🔍
</button>


The visible button may contain only a symbol, but assistive technology
can announce:

Search


USE ARIA-LABEL WHEN
-------------------

An element needs an accessible name and there is no useful visible text
that can provide that name.


IMPORTANT
---------

The aria-label value should be human-readable.

Good:

aria-label="Registration help"


Poor:

aria-label="registration-help-button-1"


Remember:

The value may be announced to an actual user.


ARIA-LABELLEDBY
===============

aria-labelledby creates an accessible name using text from another
element on the page.

Example:

<section aria-labelledby="registration-heading">

    <h2 id="registration-heading">
        Workshop Registration
    </h2>

</section>


Relationship:

section

aria-labelledby="registration-heading"

        ↓

id="registration-heading"


The visible heading becomes the accessible name for the section.


ARIA-LABEL VS ARIA-LABELLEDBY
-----------------------------

aria-label

Accessible name is written directly in the attribute.


aria-labelledby

Accessible name comes from another element.


MENTAL MODEL
------------

No useful visible label exists:

aria-label


Useful visible text already exists:

aria-labelledby


NATIVE LABEL ALREADY EXISTS:

Do not add unnecessary ARIA.


Example:

<label for="name">Name</label>
<input id="name" name="name">


The input already has an accessible name.

You normally do NOT need:

aria-label="Name"

or:

aria-labelledby="..."


ARIA-HIDDEN
===========

aria-hidden="true"

removes an element from the accessibility tree.

The content may still remain visually displayed.


DECORATIVE CONTENT
------------------

Example:

<button type="button">

    <span aria-hidden="true">
        →
    </span>

    Continue

</button>


The arrow is decorative.

The word:

Continue

already communicates the button's purpose.


OTHER EXAMPLES
--------------

Decorative stars:

<span aria-hidden="true">★</span>


Decorative calendar symbol:

<span aria-hidden="true">📅</span>

September 22, 2026


The actual date still communicates the information.


DO NOT HIDE MEANINGFUL INFORMATION
----------------------------------

Bad idea:

<span aria-hidden="true">
    Warning:
</span>


If "Warning" communicates important meaning, assistive technology
should normally be able to access it.


DO NOT HIDE INTERACTIVE CONTENT
-------------------------------

Avoid placing:

aria-hidden="true"

on:

- links
- buttons
- inputs
- other focusable interactive controls


ARIA-DESCRIBEDBY
================

aria-describedby associates an element with additional descriptive
information.


Example:

<label for="password">
    Password
</label>

<input
    type="password"
    id="password"
    name="password"
    aria-describedby="password-help"
>

<p id="password-help">
    Password must contain at least eight characters.
</p>


Relationship:

input

aria-describedby="password-help"

        ↓

p id="password-help"


NAME VS DESCRIPTION
-------------------

The label answers:

"What is this control?"


Example:

Password


aria-describedby answers:

"What additional information should I know about this control?"


Example:

Password must contain at least eight characters.


MENTAL MODEL
------------

<label>
    ↓
ACCESSIBLE NAME


aria-describedby
    ↓
ACCESSIBLE DESCRIPTION


ALT TEXT
========

The alt attribute provides a text alternative for an image.


INFORMATIVE IMAGES
------------------

If an image communicates useful information, give it meaningful alt
text.

Example:

<img
    src="conference-room.jpg"
    alt="Attendees seated at tables during a web accessibility workshop"
>


Good alt text communicates the important meaning or purpose of the
image.


AVOID REDUNDANT PHRASES
-----------------------

Usually avoid phrases such as:

Image of...
Picture of...
Photo of...


Screen readers already know the element is an image.


DECORATIVE IMAGES
-----------------

If an image is purely decorative and provides no useful information,
use an empty alt attribute:

alt=""


Example:

<img
    src="decorative-divider.png"
    alt=""
>


Do not simply omit alt from an image that should intentionally be
ignored.


CONTEXT MATTERS
---------------

The same image may need different alt text depending on why it appears
on the page.

Ask:

"What information would someone lose if they could not see this image?"


ACCESSIBLE LINK TEXT
====================

Link text should clearly describe the destination or purpose of the
link.


POOR LINK TEXT
--------------

Click here

Read more

Learn more

More

Download


These phrases may make little sense when read without surrounding
content.


BETTER LINK TEXT
----------------

Workshop agenda

Download the accessibility checklist

Learn about accessible HTML

W3C web accessibility guidance

Email the workshop team


LINKS OUT OF CONTEXT
--------------------

Screen reader users may navigate through a list of links without
hearing the surrounding paragraphs.

Ask:

"If I heard only this link text, would I understand what the link does
or where it goes?"


AVOID
-----

Avoid adding unnecessary phrases such as:

Link to...

Click this link to...


The anchor element already communicates that the content is a link.


ACCESSIBLE AUDIO AND VIDEO
==========================

Multimedia content should provide alternatives for users who cannot
hear or see parts of the media.


AUDIO
-----

For meaningful spoken audio, provide a text transcript.

A transcript should contain the important information that is actually
spoken.

A sentence saying:

"This audio discusses accessibility."

is NOT equivalent to the actual spoken content.


VIDEO CAPTIONS
--------------

Captions provide text for spoken dialogue and important sounds.

HTML video can use the <track> element.

Example structure:

<video controls>

    <source
        src="training.mp4"
        type="video/mp4"
    >

    <track
        src="training-en.vtt"
        kind="captions"
        srclang="en"
        label="English"
        default
    >

    Your browser does not support the video element.

</video>


IMPORTANT TRACK ATTRIBUTES
--------------------------

src
Location of the text track file.

kind
Identifies the type of track.

Example:

kind="captions"


srclang
Identifies the language.

Example:

srclang="en"


label
Human-readable name for the track.

Example:

label="English"


default
Makes that track the initially preferred track.


TRANSCRIPTS
-----------

A transcript provides the media content as readable text.

Transcripts are especially important for audio-only content.


AUDIO DESCRIPTIONS
------------------

Video may also require descriptions of important visual information
that cannot be understood from the audio alone.


NATIVE MEDIA CONTROLS
---------------------

Use:

controls

when users need to operate audio or video.

Example:

<audio controls>

<video controls>


Native controls provide established playback functionality.


KEYBOARD ACCESSIBILITY
======================

A webpage should be usable without requiring a mouse.

Users should normally be able to navigate interactive elements using:

Tab
Shift + Tab
Enter
Space


NATURALLY FOCUSABLE ELEMENTS
----------------------------

Native interactive HTML elements normally participate in keyboard
navigation automatically.

Examples:

<a href="...">

<button>

<input>

<select>

<textarea>


These generally do NOT require tabindex simply to become keyboard
accessible.


TABINDEX
========

The tabindex attribute controls whether an element can receive focus
and how it participates in keyboard navigation.


TABINDEX="0"
------------

Makes an element participate in the normal sequential tab order.

Example:

<div tabindex="0">
    ...
</div>


Use carefully.

Do not make ordinary static content focusable without a good reason.


TABINDEX="-1"
-------------

Allows an element to receive programmatic focus but removes it from
normal sequential Tab navigation.

Example:

<p tabindex="-1">
    Sorry, there was an error with your submission.
</p>


This can be useful when focus needs to be moved to an element through
application behavior without making it a normal tab stop.


POSITIVE TABINDEX
-----------------

Avoid:

tabindex="1"

tabindex="2"

tabindex="3"


Positive values manually override the natural keyboard order and can
create confusing navigation.


BEST PRACTICE
-------------

Prefer:

good document order
+
native HTML
+
natural browser tab order


Instead of manually numbering every interactive element.


EXAMPLE OF A GOOD NATURAL TAB ORDER
-----------------------------------

nav link
    ↓
nav link
    ↓
name input
    ↓
email input
    ↓
submit button
    ↓
contact link


No positive tabindex values are necessary.


ACCESSKEY
=========

The accesskey attribute can define a keyboard shortcut for an element.

Example:

<button accesskey="s">
    Save
</button>


Another example:

<a href="/" accesskey="h">
    Home
</a>


Accessibility shortcut behavior can vary between browsers and
operating systems, so use accesskey carefully.


SEMANTIC HTML AND ACCESSIBILITY
===============================

Accessibility frequently starts with choosing the correct HTML element.

Examples:

Need a button?
→ use <button>

Need navigation?
→ use <nav>

Need the main page content?
→ use <main>

Need a page heading?
→ use <h1>

Need a form label?
→ use <label>

Need tabular data?
→ use <table>

Need contact information?
→ consider <address>

Need a list?
→ use <ul> or <ol>


Native HTML gives browsers and assistive technologies useful semantic
information automatically.


ACCESSIBILITY AUDIT WORKFLOW
============================

A practical workflow:

Build page

    ↓

Validate HTML

    ↓

Open page in browser

    ↓

Inspect DOM with DevTools

    ↓

Run accessibility audit

    ↓

Test keyboard navigation

    ↓

Check headings and landmarks

    ↓

Check forms and labels

    ↓

Check images and alt text

    ↓

Check tables

    ↓

Check links

    ↓

Check ARIA

    ↓

Check multimedia alternatives

    ↓

Fix source HTML

    ↓

Retest


AUTOMATED TESTING LIMITATIONS
=============================

An automated audit may tell you:

- an image has no alt attribute
- a form control has no label
- heading levels are problematic
- ARIA references are invalid
- color contrast is insufficient
- certain landmark problems exist


But an automated tool cannot always determine whether:

- alt text is actually useful
- link wording makes sense
- heading wording accurately describes content
- tab order feels logical
- ARIA was the correct solution
- a transcript accurately represents an audio recording


Human review is still necessary.


ACCESSIBILITY QUICK REFERENCE
=============================

WCAG
Web Content Accessibility Guidelines


POUR
Perceivable
Operable
Understandable
Robust


<label>
Accessible name for a form control


alt
Text alternative for an image


scope="col"
Header describes cells below it


scope="row"
Header describes cells beside it


aria-label
Provides an accessible name directly


aria-labelledby
Uses another element's text as the accessible name


aria-describedby
Associates additional descriptive information


aria-hidden="true"
Removes content from the accessibility tree


controls
Provides native audio/video playback controls


<track>
Provides captions or other timed text for media


tabindex="0"
Adds an element to normal keyboard navigation


tabindex="-1"
Removes an element from sequential Tab navigation while allowing
programmatic focus


tabindex greater than 0
Avoid


accesskey
Provides a keyboard shortcut


MAIN ACCESSIBILITY RULES
========================

1. USE NATIVE HTML FIRST

Do not recreate native functionality with ARIA when HTML already has
the correct element.


2. PROVIDE ACCESSIBLE NAMES

Users need to know what controls do.


3. PROVIDE ACCESSIBLE DESCRIPTIONS WHEN NEEDED

Instructions and help text should be programmatically associated with
the correct controls.


4. DO NOT HIDE MEANINGFUL INFORMATION

Use aria-hidden only when removing the content from the accessibility
tree does not remove important meaning.


5. WRITE DESCRIPTIVE LINK TEXT

Links should make sense without surrounding context.


6. PROVIDE TEXT ALTERNATIVES

Images, audio, and video should have appropriate alternatives.


7. PRESERVE KEYBOARD ACCESS

Users should be able to reach and operate interactive content without
a mouse.


8. USE LOGICAL HEADINGS

Headings describe document structure, not visual formatting.


9. BUILD ACCESSIBLE TABLE RELATIONSHIPS

Use headers, scope, captions, and proper table structure.


10. TEST ACCESSIBILITY

Do not assume valid HTML automatically means a page is fully
accessible.


FINAL MENTAL MODEL
==================

Good accessibility usually begins with:

Semantic HTML
      +
Logical document structure
      +
Accessible names
      +
Accessible descriptions
      +
Text alternatives
      +
Keyboard support
      +
Appropriate ARIA
      +
Testing


KEY TAKEAWAY
============

Accessibility is not a single HTML attribute or tool.

It is the practice of building webpages so users can perceive,
understand, navigate, and operate the content using different devices,
input methods, and assistive technologies.

Start with native semantic HTML.

Add ARIA only when it provides accessibility information that native
HTML does not already provide.

Then test the finished experience rather than assuming the markup alone
is sufficient.


HTML REVIEW
===========

RESPONSIVE WEB DESIGN — HTML REVIEW NOTES


HTML BASICS
===========

HTML
----

HTML stands for:

HyperText Markup Language

HTML defines the structure and meaning of webpage content.

HTML is responsible primarily for:

- page structure
- content organization
- semantic meaning

CSS is primarily responsible for presentation.

JavaScript is primarily responsible for behavior and interactivity.


HTML ELEMENTS
-------------

HTML elements represent content on a webpage.

Most HTML elements consist of:

opening tag
+
content
+
closing tag


Example:

<p>Hello world</p>


Structure:

<p>
    ↓
opening tag

Hello world
    ↓
content

</p>
    ↓
closing tag


COMMON HTML ELEMENTS
--------------------

Heading:

<h1>Page Title</h1>


Paragraph:

<p>This is a paragraph.</p>


Generic container:

<div>
    Content
</div>


Button:

<button type="button">
    Open Menu
</button>


DIV ELEMENT
-----------

<div>

The div element is a generic container.

It does NOT provide special semantic meaning.

Use a div when:

- grouping content is necessary
- no more appropriate semantic HTML element exists


Do not automatically use div for everything.

Prefer semantic elements when they accurately describe the content.


VOID ELEMENTS
=============

Void elements do not have closing tags.

Examples:

<img>

<input>

<meta>

<link>

<br>

<hr>

<source>

<track>


Example:

<img
    src="cat.jpg"
    alt="Orange cat sleeping on a chair"
>


Do NOT write:

<img></img>


HTML ATTRIBUTES
===============

Attributes provide additional information or behavior for HTML
elements.

General syntax:

<element attribute="value">


Example:

<a href="https://example.com">
    Visit Example
</a>


Common attributes include:

id
class
href
src
alt
name
type
value
required
disabled
readonly
title
lang


BOOLEAN ATTRIBUTES
------------------

Some attributes represent true/false states.

Examples:

required

disabled

readonly

checked

selected

controls

default

muted


For example:

<input type="text" required>


The presence of required means the attribute is enabled.


IDS AND CLASSES
===============

ID
--

The id attribute uniquely identifies an element within a document.

Example:

<section id="registration">

An id should normally appear only once on a page.


IDs are useful for:

- internal links
- label relationships
- ARIA relationships
- CSS targeting
- JavaScript targeting


Example internal relationship:

<a href="#contact">Contact</a>

<section id="contact">
    ...
</section>


ID VALUES
---------

IDs cannot contain spaces.

Multiple words are commonly separated with:

-

Example:

id="contact-information"


Underscores may also be used in valid id values.


CLASS
-----

The class attribute groups elements together.

Unlike an id, the same class can be used on multiple elements.

Example:

<p class="important">
    ...
</p>

<p class="important">
    ...
</p>


An element can also contain multiple classes:

class="card featured"


HTML ENTITIES
=============

HTML entities allow special characters to be represented safely in
HTML.

Examples:

&amp;
→ &


&lt;
→ <


&gt;
→ >


&quot;
→ "


Useful when a character could otherwise be interpreted as HTML markup.


HTML BOILERPLATE
================

A basic HTML document generally begins with:

<!DOCTYPE html>

<html lang="en">

<head>
    ...
</head>

<body>
    ...
</body>

</html>


DOCTYPE
-------

<!DOCTYPE html>

Tells the browser that the document uses modern HTML.


HTML ELEMENT
------------

<html>

The root element containing the HTML document.


LANG ATTRIBUTE
--------------

Example:

<html lang="en">


Identifies the primary language of the page.

This helps:

- screen readers
- translation tools
- search engines
- browsers


HEAD ELEMENT
------------

<head>

Contains document metadata and resources that generally are not
displayed as normal page content.


BODY ELEMENT
------------

<body>

Contains the content presented to the user.


CHARACTER ENCODING
==================

UTF-8 is commonly used for HTML documents.

Example:

<meta charset="UTF-8">


UTF-8 supports a very large collection of international characters
and symbols.


VIEWPORT META TAG
=================

Common responsive-webpage metadata:

<meta
    name="viewport"
    content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"
>


This helps pages render appropriately on different screen sizes.


TITLE ELEMENT
=============

<title>

Defines the document title.

Example:

<title>Community Workshop</title>


The title commonly appears in:

- browser tabs
- bookmarks
- search engine results


META DESCRIPTION
================

A description meta tag provides a short description of the webpage.

Example:

<meta
    name="description"
    content="Registration information for a community HTML workshop."
>


A useful meta description can help search engines understand and
present a page.


OPEN GRAPH TAGS
===============

Open Graph metadata can control how a webpage appears when shared on
social media or messaging platforms.

Common Open Graph properties include:

og:title

og:description

og:image

og:url


LINK ELEMENT
============

<link>

Can associate external resources with the document.

Common use:

<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">


SCRIPT ELEMENT
==============

<script>

Can load or contain JavaScript.

Example:

<script src="script.js"></script>


PATHS
=====

ABSOLUTE PATH / URL
-------------------

An absolute URL provides the full address to an external resource.

Example:

https://example.com/images/photo.jpg


RELATIVE PATH
-------------

A relative path identifies a resource relative to the current file.

Example:

images/photo.jpg


CURRENT DIRECTORY
-----------------

./

Example:

./images/photo.jpg


PARENT DIRECTORY
----------------

../

Example:

../images/photo.jpg


ROOT-RELATIVE PATH
------------------

A leading slash refers to a path beginning at the site's root.

Example:

/images/photo.jpg


ANCHOR ELEMENT
==============

<a>

Creates a hyperlink.

Example:

<a href="https://example.com">
    Visit Example
</a>


HREF
----

The href attribute specifies the destination.


INTERNAL LINKS
--------------

Links can jump to elements on the same page.

Example:

<a href="#registration">
    Registration
</a>


Destination:

<section id="registration">
    ...
</section>


The fragment:

#registration

references:

id="registration"


MAILTO LINKS
------------

Example:

<a href="mailto:workshops@example.com">
    Email the workshop team
</a>


TEL LINKS
---------

Example:

<a href="tel:+15551234567">
    Call the workshop team
</a>


TARGET ATTRIBUTE
================

The target attribute specifies where a linked resource opens.

Important values include:

_self
_blank
_parent
_top


_self
-----

Opens in the current browsing context.


_blank
------

Usually opens in a new tab or window.


_parent
-------

Opens in the parent browsing context.


_top
----

Opens in the top-level browsing context.


LINK STATES
===========

Links can have different interaction states.

Common states include:

unvisited

visited

hover

focus

active


CSS can later style these states differently.


IMAGES
======

The <img> element embeds an image.

Example:

<img
    src="photo.jpg"
    alt="Students working together at a web development workshop"
>


SRC
---

Specifies the image resource.


ALT
---

Provides a text alternative.

Alt text is important for accessibility.


INFORMATIVE IMAGE
-----------------

Provide meaningful alt text.

Example:

alt="Instructor demonstrating HTML on a projector"


DECORATIVE IMAGE
----------------

Use:

alt=""


when an image provides no meaningful information and should be ignored
by assistive technology.


REPLACED ELEMENTS
=================

Some HTML elements are replaced by external content.

Common examples include:

<img>

<video>

<iframe>

<embed>


The browser displays the external resource in place of the element.


IMAGE FORMATS
=============

Common web image formats include:

JPEG / JPG

PNG

GIF

SVG

WebP

AVIF


Different formats have different strengths.

For example:

JPEG
→ often useful for photographs

PNG
→ supports transparency

SVG
→ scalable vector graphics

WebP / AVIF
→ modern formats designed for efficient web delivery


SVG
===

SVG stands for:

Scalable Vector Graphics

SVG graphics can scale without losing sharpness because they are based
on vector shapes rather than a fixed pixel grid.


MEDIA LICENSING
===============

Images and other media may be protected by copyright.

Before using media, confirm you have permission or that its license
allows your intended use.


AUDIO
=====

The <audio> element embeds audio.

Example:

<audio controls>

    <source
        src="music.mp3"
        type="audio/mpeg"
    >

    Your browser does not support the audio element.

</audio>


VIDEO
=====

The <video> element embeds video.

Example:

<video controls>

    <source
        src="training.mp4"
        type="video/mp4"
    >

    Your browser does not support the video element.

</video>


COMMON MEDIA ATTRIBUTES
=======================

controls
--------

Provides native playback controls.


autoplay
--------

Requests that playback begin automatically.

Autoplay should be used carefully because unexpected media playback
can create usability and accessibility problems.


loop
----

Repeats the media after it finishes.


muted
-----

Starts the media without sound.


poster
------

Video only.

Provides an image displayed before playback begins.

Example:

<video poster="preview.jpg">


SOURCE ELEMENT
==============

<source>

Allows media resources to be specified inside audio or video
elements.

Example:

<source
    src="training.mp4"
    type="video/mp4"
>


IFRAME
======

<iframe>

Embeds another webpage or external resource within the current page.

Commonly used for embedded videos and maps.

Example:

<iframe
    src="..."
    title="Introduction to HTML video"
></iframe>


An iframe should have a meaningful title when necessary for
accessibility.


SEMANTIC HTML
=============

Semantic HTML uses elements whose names communicate the purpose or
meaning of their content.

Examples:

<header>

<nav>

<main>

<section>

<article>

<aside>

<footer>


Semantic HTML benefits:

- accessibility
- readability
- maintainability
- search engines
- assistive technologies


HEADER
------

<header>

Represents introductory content for a page or section.


NAV
---

<nav>

Represents a major group of navigation links.


MAIN
----

<main>

Represents the primary content of the document.

A page generally has one main content region.


SECTION
-------

<section>

Groups related content into a thematic section.

Sections commonly have headings.


ARTICLE
-------

<article>

Represents self-contained content that can make sense independently.

Examples:

- blog post
- news story
- forum post
- product review


ASIDE
-----

<aside>

Represents related or supplementary content.


FOOTER
------

<footer>

Represents footer information for a page or section.


HEADING HIERARCHY
=================

HTML provides:

<h1>
<h2>
<h3>
<h4>
<h5>
<h6>


<h1>

represents the highest heading level.

<h6>

represents the lowest.


Choose heading levels based on document structure, not appearance.


Example:

<h1>Web Development Conference</h1>

<h2>Schedule</h2>

<h3>Morning Sessions</h3>

<h3>Afternoon Sessions</h3>

<h2>Registration</h2>


Avoid skipping heading levels without a structural reason.

Logical heading structure helps assistive technology users understand
and navigate the page.


PRESENTATIONAL VS SEMANTIC HTML
===============================

Older HTML sometimes used elements primarily to control appearance.

Examples of deprecated presentational elements include:

<center>

<font>

<big>


Modern HTML should describe structure and meaning.

CSS should control visual presentation.


TEXT SEMANTICS
==============

EM
--

<em>

Represents stress emphasis.

Example:

<p>
    You <em>must</em> submit the form.
</p>


STRONG
------

<strong>

Represents strong importance.

Example:

<strong>Warning:</strong>


I
-

<i>

Represents text set apart for reasons such as:

- idiomatic expression
- technical term
- alternative voice
- foreign phrase


It does not mean:

"make this text visually italic."


If you only want visual italics, CSS is more appropriate.


B
-

<b>

Draws attention to text without indicating increased importance.

It does not provide the same meaning as <strong>.


BLOCKQUOTE
==========

<blockquote>

Represents a longer quotation from another source.

Example:

<blockquote cite="https://example.com">
    ...
</blockquote>


Q
=

<q>

Represents a short inline quotation.

Example:

<p>
    The instructor said,
    <q>Use semantic HTML first.</q>
</p>


ABBR
====

<abbr>

Represents an abbreviation.

Example:

<abbr title="HyperText Markup Language">
    HTML
</abbr>


ADDRESS
=======

<address>

Represents contact information associated with a person, organization,
article, or page.

Example:

<address>
    Atlanta Web Skills Conference<br>
    Atlanta, GA
</address>


BR
==

<br>

Creates a line break.

Useful for content where the line break itself is meaningful, such as
a physical address.

Do not use repeated <br> elements merely to create page spacing.


TIME
====

<time>

Represents a date and/or time.

Example:

<time datetime="2026-09-18">
    September 18, 2026
</time>


Machine-readable value:

datetime="2026-09-18"


Date and time:

<time datetime="2026-09-18T09:00">
    September 18, 2026 at 9:00 AM
</time>


SUPERSCRIPT
===========

<sup>

Represents superscript text.

Example:

x<sup>2</sup>


SUBSCRIPT
=========

<sub>

Represents subscript text.

Example:

H<sub>2</sub>O


CODE
====

<code>

Represents a fragment of computer code.

Example:

<p>
    Use the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> element for paragraphs.
</p>


PRE
===

<pre>

Preserves whitespace and line breaks.

Often combined with <code> for multi-line examples.

Example:

<pre><code>&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Welcome.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>


U
=

<u>

Represents text with a non-textual annotation.

One possible use is marking a misspelled word.

It should not simply be used because you want underlined text.


S
=

<s>

Represents information that is no longer accurate or relevant.

Example:

<s>$49.99</s>

$29.99


RUBY
====

<ruby>

Used for ruby annotations, commonly for pronunciation information in
East Asian typography.

Related elements include:

<rt>

<rp>


DESCRIPTION LISTS
=================

<dl>

Description list.


<dt>

Term.


<dd>

Description of the term.


Example:

<dl>

    <dt>HTML</dt>

    <dd>
        HyperText Markup Language
    </dd>

</dl>


HTML FORMS
==========

<form>

Creates a form for collecting user input.


Example:

<form
    action="https://example.com/register"
    method="get"
>

    ...

</form>


ACTION
------

Specifies where form data is sent.


METHOD
------

Specifies the HTTP method used to send the data.

Common methods:

GET

POST


INPUT
=====

<input>

Creates a form control.

Common input types include:

text

email

password

number

radio

checkbox

date

submit

reset

button


TEXT INPUT
----------

<input type="text">


EMAIL INPUT
-----------

<input type="email">


Browsers can perform basic email-format validation.


PASSWORD INPUT
--------------

<input type="password">


NUMBER INPUT
------------

<input type="number">


RADIO INPUT
-----------

<input type="radio">


CHECKBOX
--------

<input type="checkbox">


INPUT ATTRIBUTES
================

ID
--

Identifies the element in the document.


NAME
----

Identifies the submitted form field.

Example:

<input
    id="email"
    name="email"
    type="email"
>


When submitted, the name becomes the field's key.


PLACEHOLDER
-----------

Displays hint text inside an input.

Example:

placeholder="name@example.com"


A placeholder should not replace a proper label.


VALUE
-----

Defines the control's value.

Example:

value="yes"


For certain button inputs, it can also define visible button text.


REQUIRED
--------

Makes a control required before form submission.

Example:

<input type="email" required>


MIN AND MAX
-----------

Define allowed numerical ranges.

Example:

<input
    type="number"
    min="0"
    max="100"
>


MINLENGTH AND MAXLENGTH
-----------------------

Define allowed character lengths.

Example:

<input
    type="text"
    minlength="5"
    maxlength="20"
>


LABELS
======

<label>

Provides an accessible name for a form control.

Example:

<label for="email">
    Email Address
</label>

<input
    type="email"
    id="email"
    name="email"
>


Relationship:

for="email"
      ↓
id="email"


The values must match.


RADIO BUTTON GROUPS
===================

Radio buttons allow one selection from a group.

Example:

<fieldset>

    <legend>
        Preferred Session
    </legend>

    <input
        type="radio"
        id="morning"
        name="session"
        value="morning"
    >
    <label for="morning">
        Morning
    </label>

    <input
        type="radio"
        id="afternoon"
        name="session"
        value="afternoon"
    >
    <label for="afternoon">
        Afternoon
    </label>

</fieldset>


Radio buttons in the same group share the same:

name


Each should have a unique:

id

value


CHECKBOX GROUPS
===============

Checkboxes allow multiple selections.

Example:

<fieldset>

    <legend>
        Topics of Interest
    </legend>

    <input
        type="checkbox"
        id="html"
        name="topics"
        value="html"
    >
    <label for="html">HTML</label>

    <input
        type="checkbox"
        id="accessibility"
        name="topics"
        value="accessibility"
    >
    <label for="accessibility">
        Accessibility
    </label>

</fieldset>


FIELDSET
========

<fieldset>

Groups related controls.


LEGEND
======

<legend>

Provides a caption for a fieldset.


BUTTONS
=======

<button>

Creates a native button.

Common button types:

submit

reset

button


SUBMIT
------

<button type="submit">
    Submit Form
</button>


RESET
-----

<button type="reset">
    Reset Form
</button>


GENERIC BUTTON
--------------

<button type="button">
    Show Information
</button>


Specify button type explicitly when possible.


SELECT
======

<select>

Creates a selection list.


OPTION
======

<option>

Represents a choice inside a select.

Example:

<label for="platform">
    Platform
</label>

<select
    id="platform"
    name="platform"
>

    <option value="pc">
        PC
    </option>

    <option value="switch">
        Nintendo Switch
    </option>

</select>


SELECTED
--------

Sets an option as initially selected.

Example:

<option value="pc" selected>
    PC
</option>


TEXTAREA
========

<textarea>

Creates a multi-line text field.

Example:

<label for="comments">
    Comments
</label>

<textarea
    id="comments"
    name="comments"
></textarea>


FORM STATES
===========

FOCUSED
-------

A control is focused when it is currently active for interaction.

Keyboard users commonly move focus using:

Tab

Shift + Tab


READONLY
--------

Prevents editing but generally keeps the field focusable and its value
available for submission.

Example:

<input
    type="text"
    value="ABC123"
    readonly
>


DISABLED
--------

Makes the control unavailable for normal interaction.

Disabled controls generally:

- are not focusable
- cannot be edited
- are not submitted with the form


Example:

<input type="text" disabled>


TABLES
======

Tables represent tabular information.

Use tables for information that naturally belongs in rows and columns.

Do NOT use tables for general page layout.


TABLE
-----

<table>

Contains the table.


CAPTION
-------

<caption>

Provides a title or description for the table.


THEAD
-----

<thead>

Groups table header rows.


TBODY
-----

<tbody>

Groups primary table data.


TFOOT
-----

<tfoot>

Groups summary/footer rows.


TR
--

<tr>

Creates a table row.


TH
--

<th>

Creates a header cell.


TD
--

<td>

Creates an ordinary data cell.


EXAMPLE STRUCTURE
-----------------

<table>

    <caption>
        Workshop Results
    </caption>

    <thead>

        <tr>
            <th>Participant</th>
            <th>Score</th>
        </tr>

    </thead>

    <tbody>

        <tr>
            <td>Jordan Parker</td>
            <td>92</td>
        </tr>

    </tbody>

</table>


COLSPAN
=======

colspan specifies how many columns a cell spans.

Example:

<th colspan="2">
    Average Score
</th>


ACCESSIBLE TABLE HEADERS
========================

scope="col"

means the header describes cells below it.


Example:

<th scope="col">
    Score
</th>


scope="row"

means the header describes cells beside it.


Example:

<th scope="row">
    Jordan Parker
</th>


ACCESSIBILITY
=============

Web accessibility means creating webpages that people with different
abilities can perceive, understand, navigate, and operate.


ASSISTIVE TECHNOLOGIES
======================

Examples include:

- screen readers
- screen magnifiers
- voice recognition software
- alternative pointing devices
- specialized keyboards


SEMANTIC HTML AND ACCESSIBILITY
===============================

Correct semantic HTML helps assistive technologies understand:

- headings
- navigation
- landmarks
- tables
- forms
- buttons
- links
- content relationships


ACCESSIBILITY AUDITING
======================

Accessibility auditing tools can detect many common issues.

Examples may include:

Google Lighthouse

WAVE

IBM Equal Access Accessibility Checker


Automated tools cannot identify every accessibility problem.

Manual testing is also important.


Useful manual checks include:

- keyboard-only navigation
- heading hierarchy
- link wording
- form labels
- alt text quality
- semantic structure


WAI-ARIA
========

WAI-ARIA stands for:

Web Accessibility Initiative —
Accessible Rich Internet Applications


ARIA can provide additional accessibility information when native HTML
does not provide everything required.


IMPORTANT ARIA RULE
-------------------

Use native HTML first.

Prefer:

<button>Register</button>


instead of recreating a button with:

<div role="button">
    Register
</div>


Native HTML automatically provides important semantics and behaviors.


ARIA ROLES
==========

ARIA roles communicate what an element represents.

Examples include roles such as:

button

menu

tab

alert


Do not add an ARIA role when a native HTML element already supplies the
correct semantics.


ARIA-LABEL
==========

aria-label provides an accessible name directly.

Example:

<button
    type="button"
    aria-label="Registration help"
>
    ?
</button>


The visible content may only be:

?

but assistive technology receives:

Registration help


ARIA-LABELLEDBY
===============

aria-labelledby gets an accessible name from another element.

Example:

<section
    aria-labelledby="registration-heading"
>

    <h2 id="registration-heading">
        Registration
    </h2>

</section>


Relationship:

aria-labelledby="registration-heading"

              ↓

id="registration-heading"


ARIA-LABEL VS ARIA-LABELLEDBY
-----------------------------

aria-label
→ write the accessible name directly


aria-labelledby
→ reference existing visible text


ARIA-DESCRIBEDBY
================

aria-describedby associates additional descriptive information with an
element.

Example:

<label for="card-number">
    Card Number
</label>

<input
    id="card-number"
    name="card-number"
    type="text"
    aria-describedby="card-number-help"
>

<p id="card-number-help">
    Enter the 16-digit number without spaces.
</p>


Mental model:

label
    ↓
WHAT IS THIS?


aria-describedby
    ↓
WHAT ELSE SHOULD I KNOW?


ARIA-HIDDEN
===========

aria-hidden="true"

removes an element from the accessibility tree while it may remain
visible.


Good use:

<button type="button">

    <span aria-hidden="true">
        →
    </span>

    Continue

</button>


The arrow is decorative.

Continue provides the meaningful accessible content.


Do not use aria-hidden on meaningful information.


Do not put aria-hidden="true" on interactive controls that users need
to access.


ARIA-EXPANDED
=============

aria-expanded communicates whether an expandable control is currently
expanded or collapsed.

Common values:

true

false


Example concept:

<button aria-expanded="false">
    Menu
</button>


ARIA-LIVE
=========

aria-live identifies content whose changes may need to be announced to
screen-reader users.

Possible uses include:

- status updates
- changing search results
- validation messages
- dynamically updated content


ALT TEXT
========

Informative images need useful alternative text.

Example:

alt="A student typing HTML code on a laptop"


Decorative images should generally use:

alt=""


Ask:

"What meaningful information would be lost if the user could not see
this image?"


GOOD LINK TEXT
==============

Link text should explain the link's purpose or destination.

Weak:

Click here

Read more

Learn more


Better:

View the workshop schedule

Download the accessibility checklist

Read W3C accessibility guidance


A useful test:

"If I heard only the link text, would I understand where it goes?"


ACCESSIBLE AUDIO
================

Meaningful audio content should have a text alternative.

For spoken audio, provide a transcript containing the important spoken
information.


ACCESSIBLE VIDEO
================

Video with meaningful audio should generally provide captions.


TRACK ELEMENT
=============

<track>

Adds timed text to media.

Example:

<video controls>

    <source
        src="training.mp4"
        type="video/mp4"
    >

    <track
        src="training-en.vtt"
        kind="captions"
        srclang="en"
        label="English"
        default
    >

    Your browser does not support video.

</video>


TRACK ATTRIBUTES
================

src
---

Location of the text-track file.


kind
----

Type of text track.

Example:

kind="captions"


srclang
-------

Language of the text track.

Example:

srclang="en"


label
-----

Human-readable name.

Example:

label="English"


default
-------

Identifies the preferred default track.


TRANSCRIPTS
===========

A transcript provides spoken media content as readable text.

A short description of what the audio discusses is NOT equivalent to a
complete transcript.


AUDIO DESCRIPTIONS
==================

Videos may require descriptions of meaningful visual information that
cannot be understood from the normal audio alone.


KEYBOARD ACCESSIBILITY
======================

Web applications should be usable without a mouse.

Common keyboard controls include:

Tab

Shift + Tab

Enter

Space


Native interactive controls are normally keyboard accessible by
default.

Examples:

<a href="...">

<button>

<input>

<select>

<textarea>


TABINDEX
========

tabindex affects focus behavior.


TABINDEX="0"
------------

Adds an element to the normal sequential tab order.

Use only when there is a real reason for a normally non-focusable
element to receive keyboard focus.


TABINDEX="-1"
-------------

Allows an element to receive programmatic focus but removes it from
normal sequential Tab navigation.


POSITIVE TABINDEX
-----------------

Avoid:

tabindex="1"

tabindex="2"

tabindex="3"


Positive values manually change keyboard order and can create confusing
navigation.


BEST PRACTICE
-------------

Prefer:

logical HTML document order
+
native interactive elements
+
natural browser focus order


ACCESSKEY
=========

accesskey defines a keyboard shortcut.

Example:

<button accesskey="s">
    Save
</button>


Browser and operating-system shortcut combinations can vary.

Accesskey should be used carefully because shortcuts can conflict with
existing browser, assistive-technology, or operating-system shortcuts.


HTML VALIDATION
===============

An HTML validator checks markup against HTML standards.

Validators can detect problems such as:

- malformed markup
- invalid nesting
- duplicate IDs
- invalid attributes
- incorrect element placement


Remember:

A browser may display invalid HTML successfully because browsers attempt
to recover from mistakes.


DOM AND DEVTOOLS
================

The DOM is the browser's parsed representation of the document.

SOURCE HTML
-----------

The HTML saved in your file.


DOM
---

The structure the browser created from that HTML.


DevTools allows you to:

- inspect elements
- inspect attributes
- inspect nesting
- temporarily edit the DOM
- investigate accessibility information
- debug links and IDs
- test possible fixes


DEVTOOLS EDITS
==============

Changes made directly in DevTools are normally temporary.

Reloading the page rebuilds the DOM from the saved source file.


DEBUGGING WORKFLOW
==================

Write HTML

    ↓

Save

    ↓

Open in browser

    ↓

Inspect with DevTools

    ↓

Test keyboard navigation

    ↓

Run accessibility audit

    ↓

Run HTML validator

    ↓

Correct source

    ↓

Reload

    ↓

Retest


