FINAL KEY TAKEAWAY
==================

Good HTML is not simply markup that displays correctly in a browser.

Good HTML communicates:

- what the content is
- how pieces of content relate to each other
- what controls do
- how users interact with them
- what information should be available to assistive technology

Start with semantic native HTML.

Use attributes to establish meaningful relationships.

Use ARIA only when native HTML does not already provide the semantics
you need.

Then validate, inspect, test with the keyboard, and audit accessibility
before considering the page finished.